NAME
       strace - graphical system call tracer

SYNOPSIS
       strace

DESCRIPTION
       Shows the system calls of a process tree live, using the kernel
       tracing facility. Start a command with the entry and Start button, or
       choose Attach to PID to pick a running process from a list. Kernel
       threads, PID 1 and the tracer itself cannot be traced, and a normal
       user can only trace their own processes. Forked children are followed
       and shown as nested nodes.

       The main area is a tree. Every process is a node with its PID, name,
       state and counters; click it, or press Left and Right, to collapse
       or expand it. Under a node are its system calls, with decoded
       arguments, the result (errors in red with the error name) and the
       duration (slow calls in yellow), and the nodes of its child processes.
       Autoscroll keeps the newest rows in view and switches off when you
       scroll up. At most 50000 rows are kept, the oldest are dropped.

       The Details tab shows all arguments of the selected call, raw and
       decoded, the string argument, result, duration and kernel timestamp.
       The Statistics tab draws a bar chart of the calls of the selected
       process; the red part of a bar is failed calls.

MENUS
       The menu bar has five menus. Click a title to open it; while a menu
       is open, moving the pointer over another title switches to it, Left
       and Right switch menus, Up, Down and Enter choose an item, and Esc or
       a click elsewhere closes it. Alt+F, Alt+E, Alt+V, Alt+T and Alt+H
       open File, Edit, View, Trace and Help. Unavailable items are grey.
       The toolbar buttons do the same as the matching menu items.

           File     Run command... (focus the command field), Attach to
                    PID..., Export JSON... (Ctrl+S), Clear, Quit (Ctrl+Q)
           Edit     Copy selected row, Copy details (to the program's own
                    clipboard), Focus filter (Ctrl+F)
           View     Toggle details panel (Ctrl+J), Autoscroll, Expand all,
                    Collapse all, Statistics
           Trace    Start, Pause display, Continue (F5), Step (F10),
                    Detach, Breakpoints...
           Help     About, man strace (shows this page)

DETAILS PANEL
       The Details and Statistics panel under the table is collapsed and
       expanded with the chevron at its left, with Ctrl+J, or from the View
       menu; clicking a tab also expands it. When it is collapsed the table
       uses the whole height.

EXPORT
       File > Export JSON... opens a save dialog with a path field filled
       in as ~/strace-PID-YYYYmmdd-HHMMSS.json and a folder list: select a
       file to use its name, double-click or press Enter on a folder to
       enter it, ".." goes up. If the file exists, Save becomes Overwrite
       and must be pressed again. The result is shown in the status bar.
       The file is streamed and has no size limit; it contains all rows
       still held by the program (at most 50000).

       JSON layout (all strings are escaped, control characters as \u00XX):

           {
             "tool": "strace", "version": "1.1.0",
             "exported_at": "2026-11-26T10:00:00Z",
             "traced": {"command": "ls /etc", "pid": 12},
             "processes": [
               {"pid": 12, "parent": 1, "name": "ls", "alive": false,
                "exit_status": {"code": 0, "signal": 0,
                                "text": "exited with 0"},
                "calls": 40, "errors": 2}
             ],
             "events": [
               {"index": 5, "pid": 12, "tid": 12, "syscall_nr": 2,
                "syscall": "open",
                "args_raw": ["0x7ffc1000", "0x0", "0x0", "0x0", "0x0", "0x0"],
                "args_decoded": ["\"/etc\"", "O_RDONLY", "0"],
                "string": "/etc",
                "result": {"raw": -2, "text": "-2 ENOENT",
                           "error": true, "errno": "ENOENT"},
                "entry_time_ns": 1234567, "duration_ns": 8900}
             ],
             "statistics": {
               "events": 90, "events_exported": 80, "completed_calls": 40,
               "failed_calls": 2, "dropped_by_kernel": 0,
               "rows_discarded_by_viewer": 0,
               "per_process": [{"pid": 12, "syscalls": [
                  {"syscall_nr": 2, "syscall": "open", "calls": 3,
                   "errors": 1, "total_ns": 25000}]}],
               "per_syscall": []
             }
           }

       A call that had not finished has "result" and "duration_ns" null.

       Toolbar:
           Pause     freeze the display; events are still collected
           Detach    stop tracing, the processes keep running
           Clear     detach and remove all rows
           Filter    show only calls whose name or string contains the text
           Breakpoints
                     choose system calls at whose entry the process stops
           Continue  resume a stopped process (F5)
           Step      resume and stop at the next system call (F10)

       A stopped process is highlighted and its call has not run yet.
       Ctrl+Q closes the window and detaches.

SEE ALSO
       trace(1), ps(1)
